}
Gotcha: if floci says "Container already running", just run eval $(floci env).
Session 1 — S3 (cloud cabinet: bucket = cabinet, object = item)
aws s3 mb s3://uncle-george-site
aws s3 cp index.html s3://uncle-george-site/
aws s3 ls s3://uncle-george-site
aws s3 cp --recursive css s3://uncle-george-site/css        # folders: cp -r
aws s3 cp data/products.json s3://uncle-george-site/data/
aws s3 ls --recursive s3://uncle-george-site
aws s3 cp s3://uncle-george-site/data/products.json download-test/   # download = reverse cp
aws s3 rm s3://uncle-george-site/index.html
aws s3 rb s3://uncle-george-site                            # FAILS: BucketNotEmpty
aws s3 rm --recursive s3://uncle-george-site                # empty first
aws s3 rb s3://uncle-george-site                            # succeeds
Gotchas: --recursive is a flag, not a subcommand (s3 cp --recursive, never s3 --recursive). NoSuchBucket = typo in bucket name. Destination prefix decides where files land (truncated dst orphans files). rb needs an empty bucket.
Session 2 — DynamoDB (table = spreadsheet, item = row, key = row's ID)
aws dynamodb create-table --table-name products \
  --attribute-definitions AttributeName=id,AttributeType=S \
  --key-schema AttributeName=id,KeyType=HASH \
  --billing-mode PAY_PER_REQUEST
aws dynamodb put-item --table-name products \
  --item '{"id":{"S":"french-baguette"},"name":{"S":"French Baguette"},"price":{"N":"45"}}'
aws dynamodb get-item --table-name products --key '{"id":{"S":"french-baguette"}}'
aws dynamodb scan --table-name products
aws dynamodb delete-table --table-name products
Gotchas: types are S=string, N=number. get-item needs the full key. scan reads everything (no filter).
Session 3 — SQS + SNS (mailbox = one-to-one pull, megaphone = one-to-many push)
aws sqs create-queue --queue-name orders        # note QueueUrl in output
aws sqs send-message --queue-url <url> --message-body '{"order":"french-baguette","qty":2}'
aws sqs receive-message --queue-url <url>       # get a message
aws sqs delete-message --queue-url <url> --receipt-handle <handle>
aws sns create-topic --name order-alerts        # note TopicArn
aws sns publish --topic-arn <arn> --message "New order placed!"
aws sqs delete-queue --queue-url <url>
Gotcha: a received message has a ReceiptHandle — you MUST delete by that handle, not by the body.
Session 4 — SSM + Secrets Manager (notes folder vs locked safe)
aws ssm put-parameter --name /site/tax-rate --value "12" --type String
aws ssm put-parameter --name /site/email --value "bakery@example.com" --type SecureString
aws ssm get-parameter --name /site/email --with-decryption
aws secretsmanager create-secret --name prod/database-url \
  --secret-string "postgresql://admin:secret@localhost/db"
aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id prod/database-url
Gotchas: --overwrite needed to update an existing param. SecureString needs --with-decryption to read the real value.
Session 5 — IAM + STS (badge = user, team = group, rules = policy, hat = role)
aws iam create-user --user-name shop-bot
aws iam create-group --group-name read-only-team
aws iam attach-group-policy --group-name read-only-team \
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess
aws iam add-user-to-group --user-name shop-bot --group-name read-only-team
aws iam create-role --role-name dev-role \
  --assume-role-policy-document file://trust-policy.json
aws sts assume-role --role-arn arn:aws:iam::000000000000:role/dev-role --role-session-name demo
aws sts get-caller-identity
Gotchas: assume-role returns temporary Credentials — use them (via profile/env) to act as the role; plain get-caller-identity shows root.
Session 6 — EventBridge + CloudWatch (dashboard/logs + event switchboard)
aws cloudwatch put-metric-data --namespace UncleGeorge --metric-name Visits --value 1
aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace UncleGeorge --metric-name Visits \
  --start-time ... --end-time ... --period 60 --statistics Sum
aws logs create-log-group --log-group-name /uncle-george/orders
aws logs create-log-stream --log-group-name /uncle-george/orders --log-stream-name order-1
aws logs put-log-events --log-group-name /uncle-george/orders --log-stream-name order-1 \
  --log-events timestamp=...,message="order placed"
aws events put-rule --name order-placed-rule --event-pattern '{"source":["uncle-george"]}'
aws events put-targets --rule order-placed-rule --targets Id=1,Arn=<sns-topic-arn>
Gotchas: EventBridge uses put-rule (NOT create-rule). Use --no-cli-pager / --no-paginate for clean output.
Session 7 — CloudFormation (one file = many resources; create=full menu, update=only the new dish, delete=close kitchen)
stack.json (the one actually used today):
{
  "AWSTemplateFormatVersion": "2010-09-09",
  "Resources": {
    "MyBucket": {
      "Type": "AWS::S3::Bucket",
      "Properties": { "BucketName": "my-cf-bucket" }
    },
    "MyTable": {
      "Type": "AWS::DynamoDB::Table",
      "Properties": {
        "TableName": "my-cf-table",
        "AttributeDefinitions": [ { "AttributeName": "id", "AttributeType": "S" } ],
        "KeySchema": [ { "AttributeName": "id", "KeyType": "HASH" } ],
        "BillingMode": "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
      }
    },
    "MyQueue": {
      "Type": "AWS::SQS::Queue",
      "Properties": { "QueueName": "my-cf-queue" }
    },
    "MyTopic": {
      "Type": "AWS::SNS::Topic",
      "Properties": { "TopicName": "order-alerts" }
    }
  }
}
Workflow (today's run):
aws cloudformation validate-template --template-body file://stack.json
# -> {"Parameters": [], "Capabilities": [], "CapabilitiesReason": ""}

aws cloudformation create-stack --stack-name my-cf-stack --template-body file://stack.json
# -> StackId arn:aws:cloudformation:us-east-1:000000000000:stack/my-cf-stack/e618dbda-...

aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name my-cf-stack
# -> "StackStatus": "CREATE_COMPLETE"

aws cloudformation describe-stack-resources --stack-name my-cf-stack
# -> MyBucket (s3), MyTable (dynamodb), MyQueue (sqs)

# EDIT stack.json to add MyTopic (SNS order-alerts), then:
aws cloudformation update-stack --stack-name my-cf-stack --template-body file://stack.json
aws cloudformation describe-stacks --stack-name my-cf-stack
# -> "StackStatus": "UPDATE_COMPLETE"  (bucket/table/queue untouched — the IaC payoff)

aws cloudformation delete-stack --stack-name my-cf-stack
aws cloudformation list-stacks
# -> my-cf-stack -> DELETE_COMPLETE
Gotchas (today's real ones):
file:// prefix is mandatory before the path.
The file must actually exist — No such file or directory: 'stack.json' means the file isn't in the current directory. Ours lived in jsonSample/; fix with cp jsonSample/stack.json . or use file://jsonSample/stack.json.
cat file.json is a read — pasting JSON into chat never creates the file; you must save it with cat > stack.json << 'EOF' ... EOF (ends with EOF on its own line).
update-stack ≠ create-stack; poll describe-stacks until _COMPLETE.
delete-stack fails if a bucket still has data (same BucketNotEmpty rule as Session 1).
